{
  "beruf": "kita",
  "title": "Cleaning and domestic work",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Cleaning offices, hotels and homes, or working for a family as a colf, is a common first job in Italy. Some people also look after children in a family (babysitter, tata) or care for an older person (badante). This deck has products and tools, the jobs in a house or an office, hours, pay and the contract, safety with chemicals, and how to talk with the signora or your employer. With a family, ask whether to say Lei or tu: many families say tu quickly, but start with Lei.",
  "introEasy": "Do you clean houses or offices, or work for a family in Italy? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Addetto/a alle pulizie",
    "Colf (collaboratore/collaboratrice familiare)",
    "Cameriera ai piani",
    "Operatore/operatrice di impresa di pulizie",
    "Baby-sitter",
    "Tata"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "On your first day, ask how the family or the client likes things done: «Che prodotto devo usare per il pavimento?», «Da dove comincio?». It avoids most problems.",
    "Never mix candeggina with other products, above all with ammoniaca or with acid descaler: the fumes are dangerous. Open the window, wear gloves and read the label.",
    "Ask for a written contract (lettera di assunzione) and a busta paga every month. Work without a contract (lavoro nero) means no contributions, no sick pay and often problems with your permesso di soggiorno.",
    "Domestic work has its own national collective agreement (CCNL lavoro domestico), and the rules on hours, rest, ferie and pay depend on your level and on whether you live in the house. Ask the employer, a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro; this is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
